@page "/"
@using System.Reflection
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Routing
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web
@inject NavigationManager Navigation

@* The single page. There is no routing beyond it, no navigation, no settings, and no
   clipboard button anywhere: the user writes the words on paper. See CLAUDE.md rule 6. *@

<PageTitle>dice to seed</PageTitle>

@* Everything is wrapped so the mode can recolour the page by redefining --accent on this one
   element. Every control already draws from that variable, so the counter, the focus rings, the
   selected buttons and the Derive button all follow without a second set of rules. The mode is
   meant to be visible from across the room, not findable by looking for it. *@
<div class="app @ModeClass">

@if (servedFromElsewhere)
{
    <div class="banner banner-loud">
        <strong>This copy is being served from @host. Do not record a real roll log in it.</strong>
        A roll log is the seed itself, in plain text, before any hashing. Anyone who learns it
        holds the wallet. Download the release, copy it to a USB stick, and run it from an
        offline Tails session instead.
    </div>
}
else
{
    @* Loopback is necessary and not sufficient: it means nobody swapped the files on you in
       transit, and says nothing about the machine. So this states the requirement rather than
       congratulating the reader on where the page came from. Tails is not a recommendation here:
       the roll log is the seed in plaintext, and an ordinary operating system keeps things, indexes
       things and may already be compromised in ways you cannot see. *@
    <div class="banner">
        <strong>Served from @host. Use this on Tails, with the network off, and nowhere else.</strong>
        Your rolls are your seed in plain text before any hashing, so the machine decides whether
        this is safe, not the app. Tails keeps nothing: it runs in RAM and forgets everything when
        you shut down. An ordinary computer does the opposite, and cannot tell you whether it is
        already compromised. Nothing here is stored, sent or copied: no local storage, no cookies,
        no network call of any kind.
    </div>
}

<h1>dice to seed</h1>

@* THE MODE IS NOT A SETTING TUCKED INSIDE A PANEL.
   ================================================
   Two things this mode chooser must keep, both learned rather than assumed.

   It is safe to offer at all only because the two results differ in kind: seed mode renders
   words, key mode renders hex and never words. A mode whose wrong position still produces
   plausible output is the Ian Coleman "Dice versus Base 10" trap, which silently yields a
   different wallet and no warning; see the runbook below. If key mode is ever given a word
   rendering, that safety property is gone and this control becomes the same hazard.

   Colour is one of three encodings, never the only one. The wording differs, the border style
   differs (solid for the seed, dashed for the key), and the result panels differ in shape. That
   survives a grayscale photo, a projector and a reader who cannot separate the two hues. *@
<div class="modes" role="group" aria-label="What are you rolling for">
    <button type="button" class="mode mode-seed @(mode == DerivationMode.SeedPhrase ? "selected" : null)"
            aria-pressed="@(mode == DerivationMode.SeedPhrase)"
            @onclick="() => RequestMode(DerivationMode.SeedPhrase)">
        <span class="mode-title">Rolling for a seed phrase</span>
        <span class="mode-detail">BIP-39 words. The wallet itself.</span>
    </button>
    <button type="button" class="mode mode-key @(mode == DerivationMode.BackupKey ? "selected" : null)"
            aria-pressed="@(mode == DerivationMode.BackupKey)"
            @onclick="() => RequestMode(DerivationMode.BackupKey)">
        <span class="mode-title">Rolling for a backup key (k)</span>
        <span class="mode-detail">32 bytes of hex. Never words.</span>
    </button>
</div>

@* Switching mode with rolls already recorded destroys them, and the confirmation says why
   rather than asking "are you sure". One roll log must never produce both a seed and a key: on
   24 words the seed entropy IS the key, byte for byte, so a log used twice makes k identical to
   the wallet it exists to protect. Clearing on switch is how that stays unreachable here.

   The confirmation is rendered inline rather than through window.confirm, because reaching that
   needs JS interop and this app ships no JavaScript of its own to review. *@
@if (pendingMode is { } pending)
{
    <div class="banner banner-loud">
        <strong>Switching to @ModeName(pending) will clear your @rolls.Length recorded rolls.</strong>
        A single roll log must never produce both a seed phrase and a backup key. The key is the
        SHA-256 of your rolls and a 24-word seed's entropy is that same hash, so one log used
        twice makes the key identical to the wallet it is meant to protect, and the shares stop
        protecting anything. Roll a fresh log for the other purpose.
        <div class="dice-actions">
            <button type="button" class="secondary" @onclick="ConfirmModeSwitch">
                Clear @rolls.Length rolls and switch
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="secondary" @onclick="CancelModeSwitch">Keep rolling, stay here</button>
        </div>
    </div>
}

<p class="subtitle">
    @if (mode == DerivationMode.SeedPhrase)
    {
        <text>
            Converts a log of six-sided dice rolls into a BIP-39 seed phrase, using the convention
            Coldcard, SeedSigner and Krux all share for d6. It has no random number generator and
            never rolls anything for you: every value comes off your dice. Derive here, then enter
            the same rolls into a second tool and compare the words. Either order works, because
            the conversion is deterministic and any correct implementation gives the same result.
        </text>
    }
    else
    {
        <text>
            Converts a log of six-sided dice rolls into the 32-byte key that
            <code>slip39-backup</code> encrypts with and splits into shares. It is the SHA-256 of
            your rolls and nothing else, so hashing the rolls in a terminal reproduces it and you
            can confirm the tool used the dice you rolled. The runbook below has the exact command,
            with your rolls already in it.
            <strong>These must be fresh rolls, not the ones behind your seed phrase.</strong> That
            rule is not a formality: on a 24-word seed the BIP-39 entropy is that same hash, byte
            for byte, so one log used twice makes your backup key derivable from the wallet it is
            protecting. Optional, and the limit is worth knowing: this is the key that wraps your
            backup, not the file key inside the age format, which the backup tool generates itself.
        </text>
    }
</p>

@* BEFORE YOU START, ABOVE THE PAD RATHER THAN BELOW IT.
   ====================================================
   This was at the bottom of the page, which is the wrong place for advice whose whole point is
   that it has to be read first. Someone who scrolls to the dice and starts pressing has already
   missed it.

   The arithmetic is behind a disclosure, and that is the compromise: the full table is fifty lines
   and would push the dice buttons off a 900 pixel window, which is what the AppImage opens at. So
   what stays visible is the three things you can act on, and the numbers backing them are one
   click away.

   Deliberately absent, and the reason belongs here rather than anywhere else: a fairness test on
   the rolls being used. At 50 rolls a chi-squared test has almost no power, six categories and
   five degrees of freedom with an expected 8.33 per face, so about one honest session in twenty
   trips the 0.05 critical value while a real bias of p=0.20 on one face goes undetected most of
   the time. Worse, a test on the rolls about to be used invites re-rolling when it fails, which
   conditions the seed on passing a statistical test and removes part of the output space. That
   reduces entropy. Testing belongs before the ceremony, on rolls that are thrown away.

   The second disclosure below replaced the previous version of this advice, which said to test a
   die "if you want to" and left the reader to guess whether it mattered. It does not, and the
   arithmetic for saying so is in RollEntropy. *@
@* Open on load, so it is read before anything is rolled, and collapsible because it is not needed
   twice. Measured rather than guessed: with this expanded the dice pad starts at y=967 in the
   1000x900 window the AppImage opens at, which puts the primary action below the fold. Collapsed,
   the pad comes back up to y=657 and into view. Nothing here is remembered between sessions, by
   design, so it reopens each time, which is the right default for advice named "before you start". *@
<details class="panel prestart" open>
    <summary><h2>Before you start</h2></summary>
    <p class="hint">
        @* "One die" is in the headline sentence rather than in a fourth hint, because a fourth hint
           costs another two lines above the dice pad and this is one word. See the ordering
           paragraph in the fairness disclosure for what it is protecting against. *@
        <strong>Roll one die @RollEntropy.RecommendedRollsFor(WordCount.Twelve) times for twelve
        words, @RollEntropy.RecommendedRollsFor(WordCount.TwentyFour) for twenty-four.</strong> The
        vendor minimums of @DiceRolls.MinimumRollsFor(WordCount.Twelve) and
        @DiceRolls.MinimumRollsFor(WordCount.TwentyFour) still work, so an existing seed can be
        reproduced, but 99 does not reach 256 bits even with a perfect die.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
        <strong>Never re-roll a log because it looks wrong.</strong> Six 6s in a row is exactly as
        likely as any other six rolls. Discarding a log for looking non-random draws your seed from a
        smaller set than the dice offered, which is the one thing that genuinely weakens it.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
        <strong>Any ordinary die is good enough: check the die, not the log.</strong> Normal
        manufacturing bias costs about one bit in a hundred and fifty. A chipped or borrowed die is the
        real risk, and no test on your rolls can find it.
    </p>

    <details class="runbook">
        <summary>What your rolls are actually worth</summary>
        @* The vendor minimums are a rounding decision and this app used to present them as though
           they were a sufficiency proof. The 99-roll row is the uncomfortable one. Every number
           comes from RollEntropy and is pinned by RollEntropyTests, so none of it can rot. *@
        <p class="hint">
            A fair d6 carries log<sub>2</sub>(6) = 2.585 bits. <strong>Real dice</strong> is a
            measurement, not an allowance: Weldon's 315,672 recorded rolls, below.
            <strong>Floor</strong> is min-entropy with one face at a fifth, fifteen times more
            lopsided than that measurement.
        </p>
        <div class="entropy-table" role="table" aria-label="Entropy by roll count">
            <div role="row" class="entropy-head">
                <span role="columnheader">rolls</span>
                <span role="columnheader">fair</span>
                <span role="columnheader">real dice</span>
                <span role="columnheader">floor</span>
                <span role="columnheader">against</span>
            </div>
            @foreach (var (count, target, label) in EntropyRows)
            {
                <div role="row" class="@(RollEntropy.PessimisticBits(count) < target ? "entropy-short" : null)">
                    <span role="cell">@count @label</span>
                    <span role="cell">@RollEntropy.FairBits(count).ToString("0.0")</span>
                    <span role="cell">@RollEntropy.MeasuredBits(count).ToString("0.0")</span>
                    <span role="cell">@RollEntropy.PessimisticBits(count).ToString("0.0")</span>
                    <span role="cell">@target</span>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
        <p class="hint">
            So <strong>99 rolls does not reach 256 bits</strong>, by a tenth of a bit, with a perfect
            die. It is a vendor rounding, not a sufficiency proof, and the minimum is kept only so a
            seed already made with 99 rolls can still be reproduced here. Note which column that
            shortfall lives in: it is the roll count, not the dice.
        </p>
        <p class="hint">
            Bias is cheap on the average measure and expensive on the floor. The lopsided die in the
            last column costs about half a bit of Shannon entropy across fifty rolls, which is the
            "fraction of a bit" figure people quote, while costing thirteen bits of min-entropy. Real
            guessing cost sits between the two, and rolling past the minimum settles it either way.
        </p>
    </details>

    @* THE FAIRNESS CLAIM, WITH ITS NUMBERS.
       ======================================
       This section exists because the page used to dismiss the pessimistic column as "worse than
       real dice are" and leave it there. That is an assurance, not a statement, and this app has no
       business making assurances: its entire value is that everything it says can be checked.

       The substantive point, which took working the numbers to see: the risk from an ordinary die is
       not worth managing, and the risk that is worth managing is not statistical. Manufacturing bias
       costs about a bit out of a hundred and fifty. A loaded or damaged die can cost far more, and no
       amount of arithmetic on the resulting log will reveal it, because a log carries no evidence of
       the die that made it. So the advice has to be about the physical object, and a fairness test on
       the rolls is the wrong instrument twice over: too weak to find real bias, and an invitation to
       re-roll, which conditions the seed on passing a test and shrinks the output space.

       Chi-squared power, since it is what people reach for: at 60 rolls the expected count is ten a
       face, five degrees of freedom, and a face at p = 0.20 gives a noncentrality of about 0.5
       against a critical value of 11.07. It goes undetected the large majority of the time, while
       one honest log in twenty trips the 0.05 threshold anyway. *@
    @* HOW TO THROW IT.
       ================
       Asked for directly: surface, height, what to do when the die goes off the table. The app had
       nothing on any of it, which is a gap in the one part of the ceremony the app cannot do for you.

       The load-bearing claim comes from casino craps, where both dice must strike the far wall and
       that wall is lined with rubber pyramids. The rule exists to destroy whatever the thrower set
       up, which is exactly the property wanted here, and it is a published rule rather than a
       recollection. See the README for the reference.

       The off-the-table rule is the part people get wrong, and it is the same principle as never
       re-rolling a log for looking wrong: a re-throw is free, and CHOOSING to re-throw after seeing
       the value is not. So the rule is fixed in advance and applied before the face is read.

       Deliberately no height in centimetres. The observable is tumbling and a bounce, and a number
       would be invented precision. *@
    <details class="runbook">
        <summary>How to throw it, and what to do when it goes off the table</summary>
        <p class="hint">
            <strong>A hard flat surface, and make it hit something.</strong> A table rather than
            carpet, which absorbs the bounce and lets a die settle without turning over. Then throw it
            against something: a book stood on edge, the inside of a box lid, the wall behind the
            table. Casino craps requires both dice to strike the far wall, and that wall is lined with
            rubber pyramids, for exactly this reason: the bounce wrecks whatever the thrower set up. It
            is the one dice convention that exists to make a throw less controllable, so it is worth
            copying.
        </p>
        <p class="hint">
            <strong>The test is tumbling, not a height.</strong> It should turn over several times and
            bounce at least once. What defeats a throw is sliding it, spinning it flat like a top, or
            dropping it from an inch, any of which can carry the starting face all the way through. If
            you would rather not think about it: shake it in an opaque cup and tip it out. That
            guarantees the tumbling and has the side benefit that you cannot see the die until it has
            landed.
        </p>
        <p class="hint">
            <strong>Decide the off-the-table rule now, before the first throw.</strong> The rule:
            if the die leaves the surface, lands leaning against something rather than flat, or cannot
            be read cleanly, that throw does not count, and you throw again and record only the second
            one.
        </p>
        <p class="hint">
            Fixing it in advance is the whole point, because the danger is not the re-throw. A throw
            that ends up on the floor is not connected to the number it would have shown, so
            discarding it costs nothing. Choosing to discard a roll <em>after</em> reading a number you
            did not like is a different act, and it narrows the set your seed came from in exactly the
            way re-rolling a whole log does. So apply the rule before you read the face, and never
            discard a roll you have already recorded.
        </p>
        <p class="hint">
            <strong>Press the button after each throw, before the next one.</strong> Then the counter
            and the throws cannot drift apart, and an interruption cannot quietly cost you a roll. A
            log one roll short still produces a perfectly convincing seed phrase for a different
            wallet.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="runbook">
        <summary>Are ordinary dice good enough, and when to suspect one</summary>
        <p class="hint">
            Yes, and the margin is large enough to state rather than assert. The bias in normal dice
            is real, understood and small. A pipped die has its spots drilled out and filled with
            lighter paint, so the six face is missing the most mass and lands upward slightly more
            often. Raphael Weldon counted it in 1894 over
            @RollEntropy.WeldonRollCount.ToString("N0") rolls: a 5 or a 6 came up
            @((RollEntropy.WeldonHighPairFrequency * 100).ToString("0.00"))% of the time against an
            expected 33.33%. It is the largest published count on ordinary dice, and an automated
            repetition in 2009 found the same direction. Casino dice have flush pips of matched
            density for exactly this reason.
        </p>
        <p class="hint">
            Put that die through a @RollEntropy.RecommendedRollsFor(WordCount.Twelve)-roll log and it
            costs you <strong>@RollEntropy.MeasuredShannonShortfall(RollEntropy.RecommendedRollsFor(WordCount.Twelve)).ToString("0.000")
            bits</strong> on the average measure, or
            <strong>@((RollEntropy.FairBits(RollEntropy.RecommendedRollsFor(WordCount.Twelve)) - RollEntropy.MeasuredBits(RollEntropy.RecommendedRollsFor(WordCount.Twelve))).ToString("0.0"))
            bits</strong> on the min-entropy floor, which is the measure that hands an attacker
            perfect knowledge of which face is heavy. Out of
            @RollEntropy.FairBits(RollEntropy.RecommendedRollsFor(WordCount.Twelve)).ToString("0.0"),
            against a target of @RollEntropy.TargetBitsFor(WordCount.Twelve). There is no way to make
            an ordinary die matter here, which is why the app has no fairness feature.
        </p>
        <p class="hint">
            <strong>The question is provenance, not fairness.</strong> A die does not become badly
            biased through manufacturing tolerance; it becomes badly biased because someone made it
            that way or because it is damaged. Statistics will not find that for you, and a
            chi-squared test on @RollEntropy.RecommendedRollsFor(WordCount.Twelve) rolls least of all:
            with ten rolls expected per face, a face landing one time in five slips past it the large
            majority of the time, while one honest log in twenty fails it regardless. Look at the die.
        </p>
        <p class="hint">Set a die aside and use a different one when:</p>
        <ul class="hint">
            <li>it is not yours: borrowed, found loose in a drawer, handed to you, or part of a
                gambling, casino-novelty or magic set</li>
            <li>an edge or corner is chipped, rounded or worn, or one face is scuffed noticeably more
                than the others, since the worn face is the one that lands down</li>
            <li>it rocks, or does not sit flat, on a hard flat surface</li>
            <li>it is translucent and shows a bubble, an inclusion or a filled hole</li>
            <li>it is hollow, foam, wood, oversized or a promotional giveaway</li>
        </ul>
        @* THE FLOAT TEST, DEMOTED.
           ========================
           This said "the test that works is physical: dissolve salt in warm water until a die floats
           ... that finds a loaded die in a minute". Two things are wrong with that, and the first is
           the practical one: many dice do not float in saturated brine at all. In the comparison at
           gnomestew.com, "Testing The Float Test", 4 of 22 dice floated. Saturated brine is about
           1.20 g/cm3 and acrylic dice sit around there, so "keep adding salt" can be a dead end, and
           telling someone to keep going until it works is worse than telling them it might not.

           The second is that the test measures where the centre of mass is, which is related to but
           not the same as which face lands upward. The same comparison found the float test's
           predictions unsupported by rolling. That part is weak evidence in itself: 100 rolls of a
           d20 is 5 expected per face, which has almost no power, so it does not refute the test
           either. Reported here as what it is rather than adopted as a conclusion.

           So the wording keeps the test as a way to catch a grossly weighted die, says it often
           cannot be run, and stops calling it "the test that works". *@
        <p class="hint">
            A new die costs less than the time spent testing an old one. If you want to try anyway,
            floating one in saturated salt water and spinning it will show up a grossly weighted die:
            the weight settles to the bottom, so the same face keeps surfacing. Whether it floats at
            all is decided by the plastic, and the margin is thinner than the instructions usually
            admit. Saturated brine is 1.20 g/cm³, at about 26% salt by weight, which is 357 g of salt
            per litre and more than most people expect. A cheap opaque board-game die is ABS or
            polystyrene at 1.02 to 1.05, so it floats easily. A translucent acrylic die is 1.19, which
            floats by one percent, sits almost entirely submerged and settles slowly and ambiguously.
            A casino die is cellulose acetate and heavier still, so it simply sinks. The comparison
            that got 4 dice out of 22 to float was testing acrylic d20s, which is exactly the marginal
            case.
        </p>
        <p class="hint">
            And it measures where the mass sits rather than which face lands upward, which is related
            but not the same. So it is a way to catch a weight glued into a novelty die, not a fairness
            measurement.
        </p>
        @* WHY NOT JUST ROLL AND COUNT: the arithmetic, because this is the first thing everyone
           reaches for and it is hopeless at any count a person will actually roll. Elementary rather
           than a power table, so it can be checked with a calculator, and every figure comes from
           RollEntropy and is pinned by RollEntropyTests.

           The Weldon coincidence at the end is not decoration. It arrived from getting a test
           assertion wrong, and it is the whole argument in one sentence: the only solid measurement
           of ordinary dice bias comes from a Victorian throwing dice 315,672 times because that is
           roughly what the measurement costs. *@
        <p class="hint">
            <strong>Counting your own rolls cannot substitute.</strong> Over
            @RecommendedRolls rolls, a face that comes up one time in five is expected
            @RollEntropy.ExpectedFaceCount(RecommendedRolls).ToString("0")
            × 1.2 = @((RollEntropy.ExpectedFaceCount(RecommendedRolls) * 1.2).ToString("0")) times
            against a fair @RollEntropy.ExpectedFaceCount(RecommendedRolls).ToString("0"), an excess of
            @((RollEntropy.ExpectedFaceCount(RecommendedRolls) * 0.2).ToString("0")). The standard
            deviation of that count is
            @RollEntropy.FaceCountDeviation(RecommendedRolls).ToString("0.0"), so the thing you are
            looking for is
            @RollEntropy.BiasSignalInDeviations(RecommendedRolls, RollEntropy.PessimisticFaceProbability).ToString("0.0")
            deviations: smaller than the noise it has to be seen against. Getting it three deviations
            clear takes
            @RollEntropy.RollsForSignalInDeviations(3, RollEntropy.PessimisticFaceProbability).ToString("N0")
            rolls.
        </p>
        <p class="hint">
            For the bias real dice actually have, the same sum gives
            @RollEntropy.RollsForSignalInDeviations(3, RollEntropy.MeasuredTopFaceProbability).ToString("N0")
            rolls. Weldon's dataset is @RollEntropy.WeldonRollCount.ToString("N0") rolls, and that is
            not a coincidence: the reason the only solid measurement of ordinary dice bias comes from
            somebody throwing dice a quarter of a million times is that a quarter of a million rolls is
            what the measurement costs. You cannot check your die by rolling it, which is why this
            section is about looking at it.
        </p>
        <p class="hint">
            Two things matter more than which die it is. <strong>Throw it so it tumbles</strong>:
            sliding it, dropping it flat from an inch, or rolling it off a fixed grip can carry the
            starting face through, so shake it in a cup or throw it hard enough to bounce off
            something. And <strong>use one die, thrown @RecommendedRolls times</strong>, pressing the
            button after each throw. Then the order is simply the order you threw them in and there is
            nothing to get wrong.
        </p>
        @* THROWING A HANDFUL AT ONCE: THE ONE MISTAKE HERE THAT IS ARITHMETICALLY LARGE.
           =============================================================================
           This paragraph replaced advice to "rotate several dice if you have them, so a bad one
           touches only its share of the log". That advice was mitigating the wrong risk. It hedged
           manufacturing bias, worth about one bit in a hundred and fifty-five by the measurement two
           paragraphs up, by introducing an ordering question worth a third of the log. The mitigation
           cost more than the thing it mitigated, which is the sort of trade this app exists to catch.

           Every figure comes from RollEntropy and is pinned by RollEntropyTests, including the case
           for one die, where the loss is exactly zero. *@
        <p class="hint">
            <strong>If you throw a handful at once, the order has to be real.</strong> Five identical
            dice landed in a heap have @RollEntropy.OrderedThrowBits(5).ToString("0.0") bits in them
            only if you can say which die is which. If you cannot, what you recorded is the set of
            faces rather than a sequence, and there are only @RollEntropy.UnorderedOutcomes(5) of
            those: @RollEntropy.UnorderedThrowBits(5).ToString("0.0") bits, a
            @((RollEntropy.FractionLostWithoutOrder(5) * 100).ToString("0"))% loss. A
            @RecommendedRolls-roll log thrown that way carries
            @RollEntropy.BitsWithoutOrder(RecommendedRolls, 5).ToString("0.0") bits against a target
            of @RollEntropy.TargetBitsFor(wordCount), while the counter reads @RecommendedRolls and
            the words look exactly as convincing. Nothing here can detect it. So use dice you can tell
            apart, read them in a fixed order every time, and never sort them into ascending order,
            which is the same mistake performed deliberately.
        </p>
        <p class="hint">
            The honest limit on all of it: a roll log carries no evidence of the die that produced it,
            so none of this is checkable afterwards, by you or by anyone. It is advice about a physical
            object, which is why it is here as words and not as a feature.
        </p>
    </details>
</details>

<div class="panel">
    @* One piece of state behind both modes, because it decides the same thing in both: how many
       rolls the log needs. Twelve words and a 128-bit key want 50; twenty-four and 256 want 99.
       Only the wording changes, so the minimum can never drift between the two. *@
    <div class="field">
        <span class="field-label">@(mode == DerivationMode.SeedPhrase ? "Words" : "Key strength")</span>
        <div class="choice">
            @foreach (var option in new[] { WordCount.Twelve, WordCount.TwentyFour })
            {
                <button type="button"
                        class="@(wordCount == option ? "selected" : null)"
                        @onclick="() => SelectWordCount(option)">
                    @* The recommended count, matching the counter's target. Quoting the vendor
                       minimum here would set an expectation the counter then contradicts. *@
                    @if (mode == DerivationMode.SeedPhrase)
                    {
                        <text>@((int)option) words (@RollEntropy.RecommendedRollsFor(option) rolls)</text>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <text>@((int)DiceRolls.StrengthFor(option))-bit (@RollEntropy.RecommendedRollsFor(option) rolls)</text>
                    }
                </button>
            }
        </div>
        @if (mode == DerivationMode.BackupKey)
        {
            <p class="hint">
                Match whatever your seed phrase is. The key is 32 bytes either way, so this
                changes only how much is behind it: 256 bits over a 12-word seed protects nothing
                the seed is not already giving away, and 128 bits under a 24-word seed makes the
                key the weakest thing in the backup.
            </p>
        }
    </div>

    @* THESE BUTTONS RECORD A ROLL. THEY DO NOT MAKE ONE.
       ==================================================
       Each one appends the face the user physically rolled and read off a real die. There is
       no handler here that picks a value, and there must never be one: a button showing a die
       face is exactly what a future reader might wire to a random pick, and that single change
       would turn this app from a verifier into a browser-based key generator. See CLAUDE.md
       rule 1, which is enforced by a test that fails the build.

       Replacing a text box with six buttons removes a whole class of failure rather than
       guarding against it: no character outside 1 to 6 can exist, nothing needs filtering,
       there is no caret to lose, and the count is exactly what was clicked. *@
    <div class="field">
        <span class="field-label">Record each roll</span>
        @* Keyboard entry for anyone who would rather not reach for the mouse fifty times:
           1 to 6 record a roll, Backspace undoes the last. The handler sits on the group so it
           catches keys whether focus is on the group itself or on one of the buttons, which is
           where focus lands as soon as anything is clicked. *@
        <div class="dice" role="group" tabindex="0" autofocus
             aria-label="Record the face you rolled" @onkeydown="HandleKey">
            @foreach (var face in Faces)
            {
                <button type="button" class="die" @onclick="() => RecordRoll(face)"
                        aria-label="@($"Record a roll of {face}")">
                    <span class="pips pips-@face" aria-hidden="true">
                        @for (var pip = 0; pip < face; pip++)
                        {
                            <span class="pip"></span>
                        }
                    </span>
                    <span class="face-number">@face</span>
                </button>
            }
        </div>
        <div class="dice-actions">
            <button type="button" class="secondary" @onclick="UndoLastRoll" disabled="@(rolls.Length == 0)">
                Undo last roll
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="secondary" @onclick="ClearRolls" disabled="@(rolls.Length == 0)">
                Clear all
            </button>
        </div>
        <p class="hint">
            Roll a real die, read the face, press the matching button. The keys 1 to 6 do the
            same thing, and Backspace undoes the last roll. Nothing here rolls for you: every
            value comes off your dice, which is the entire point of the app. Write each throw on
            <a href="@(SheetBaseName).html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the printed sheet</a>
            (<a href="@(SheetBaseName).pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PDF</a>) as well: comparing it
            against the screen at the end is the only thing that catches a mis-press.
        </p>
    </div>

    @* The roll counter is the largest element on the page on purpose: miscounting the log is
       the most common error in the whole ceremony, and a log one roll short still produces a
       perfectly plausible seed. *@
    <div class="field">
        <span class="field-label">Rolls</span>
        <div class="counter @CounterClass">
            @* The denominator is the recommended count, not the vendor minimum: the usual job here
               is making a new seed, so the target on screen should be the number that is actually
               sufficient. Derive unlocks earlier, at the minimum, and the note says so. *@
            @RollCount<span class="of"> / @RecommendedRolls</span>
        </div>
        <div class="counter-note">@CounterNote</div>
    </div>

    <div class="field">
        <span class="field-label">What will be hashed</span>
        <pre class="mono-block @(LivePreimage.Length == 0 ? "empty" : null)">@(LivePreimage.Length == 0 ? "(nothing yet)" : LivePreimage)</pre>
        <p class="hint">
            No separator, no trailing newline, nothing appended. Compare this string character
            for character with what any other tool is hashing.
        </p>

        @* CHECKING THE RECORDED LOG AGAINST THE PAPER.
           ===========================================
           The exact preimage above is untouched and stays that way: rule 4 requires it rendered
           character for character, and this is a second view of the same value, never a replacement.

           Why it is here at all. Every verification this app offers compares the log against another
           implementation of the conversion, which means none of them can see a mis-press. Press 4
           where the die showed 5 and both tools agree perfectly, the counter still reads sixty, and
           the words are valid BIP-39 for a wallet your dice did not make. The only defence is an
           independent note of what the dice showed, and the app was asking people to keep one (in
           TAILS_INSTRUCTIONS) without ever asking them to compare it, which is the cost of a second
           plaintext copy of the seed with none of the benefit.

           Rows of ten because comparing sixty undifferentiated digits is itself an exercise in losing
           your place, which is the error being hunted. The row numbers are CSS generated content on a
           data attribute, the same device the backup key uses, so selecting this block copies digits
           and never the numbering. *@
        @if (LivePreimage.Length > 0 && ParsedLog.IsSuccess)
        {
            <div class="roll-rows" aria-label="Recorded rolls in rows of ten">
                @foreach (var row in ParsedLog.Value.RowsOfTen)
                {
                    <span class="roll-row" data-index="@row.FirstRoll">@row.Digits</span>
                }
            </div>
            <p class="hint">
                <strong>Check these against your paper before pressing Derive.</strong> A mis-pressed
                button is the one mistake nothing else here can find: every other check compares your
                log against a second tool, and two tools given the same wrong log agree perfectly.
                <a href="@(SheetBaseName).html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Print the sheet</a>
                (<a href="@(SheetBaseName).pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PDF</a>), ruled in these same
                rows of ten, before you start. Then destroy the paper: it is the wallet in
                plain text, and it is not a backup.
            </p>
        }
    </div>

    <button class="derive" disabled="@(!CanDerive)" @onclick="RunDerivation">Derive</button>

    @if (error is not null)
    {
        <p class="error">@error</p>
    }
</div>

@if (derivation is not null)
{
    <h2>Result</h2>
    <div class="panel">
        @if (ShortOfRecommended)
        {
            <div class="warn-block">@RecommendationWarning</div>
        }

        <div class="step-label">1. What was hashed: your rolls, and nothing else</div>
        <pre class="mono-block">@derivation.Preimage</pre>

        <div class="step-label">2. SHA-256 of that string</div>
        <pre class="mono-block">@derivation.Sha256Hex</pre>

        <div class="step-label">3. Entropy: the first @(derivation.EntropyHex.Length / 2) bytes of that hash, truncated, not re-hashed</div>
        <pre class="mono-block">@derivation.EntropyHex</pre>

        <div class="step-label">4. BIP-39 words</div>
        <ol class="words">
            @foreach (var word in derivation.Words)
            {
                <li>@word</li>
            }
        </ol>
        <p class="hint">
            @* The old justification was that the app cannot tell whether this machine is the
               amnesic offline one. That reasoning is retired: the requirement is Tails, stated at
               the top of the page, and hedging about where you might be undercuts it. The reason
               to write on paper is simpler and does not depend on the machine at all. *@
            Write these on paper, now, and check them against the screen before you leave the page.
            Paper is what survives the session; Tails forgets everything when it shuts down, and
            that is what you chose it for.
        </p>
    </div>
}

@* The key result deliberately looks nothing like the seed result: dashed border, hex in
   numbered groups instead of a numbered word list, and a heading that says what it is not.
   Someone glancing at the wrong screen, or at a photograph of one, has to be unable to mistake
   these for each other. *@
@if (backupKey is not null)
{
    <h2>Result</h2>
    <div class="panel panel-key">
        @if (ShortOfRecommended)
        {
            <div class="warn-block">@RecommendationWarning</div>
        }

        <div class="step-label">1. What was hashed: your rolls, and nothing else</div>
        <pre class="mono-block">@backupKey.Preimage</pre>

        @* TWO RENDERINGS OF ONE VALUE, AND THE COPYABLE ONE COMES FIRST.
           =============================================================
           The grouped view was the only rendering, and it cannot be copied: selecting it drags the
           group numbers in, so you get "1 6cb0 2 9af8 3 5505" instead of the key. Anyone pasting
           that into the backup tool would be pasting rubbish, and the check code would catch it,
           which is a poor substitute for not causing it.

           So the unbroken string is the primary form and is labelled as the thing that goes to the
           backup tool, and the grouped view stays underneath as a reading aid for writing it by
           hand. The group numbers carry user-select: none, so even a careless selection of the
           grouped block no longer picks them up. *@
        <div class="step-label">2. SHA-256 of that string. This is the key</div>
        <div class="handoff">
            <div class="handoff-label">This is what goes into <code>slip39-backup</code></div>
            <pre class="mono-block key-plain">@backupKey.KeyHex</pre>
            <div class="handoff-label">And this, as the check code</div>
            <div class="check-code">@backupKey.CheckCode</div>
        </div>
        <p class="hint">
            Those two values, and nothing else on this page. All 32 bytes of the hash, as 64
            characters, plus the four-character code.
        </p>

        <div class="step-label">The same key in groups, for writing it out by hand</div>
        <div class="key-groups">
            @foreach (var (group, index) in backupKey.KeyHexGroups.Select((g, i) => (g, i)))
            {
                @* The group number is a CSS ::before on data-index, not a text node. user-select:
                   none was tried first and is not enough: it stops a mouse selecting the number but
                   the text is still in the DOM, so a copy can pick it up and you paste
                   "1 6cb0 2 9af8" instead of the key. Generated content is never in the DOM text,
                   so no copy of any kind can reach it. *@
                <span class="key-group" data-index="@(index + 1)">@group</span>
            }
        </div>
        <p class="hint">
            Identical to the line above, in sixteen groups of four. Sixty-four unbroken characters is
            where people lose their place, and the numbers let you resume after an interruption. If
            you are copying and pasting, use the single line above: this one is for pen and paper.
        </p>

        <div class="step-label">About the check code</div>
        <p class="hint">
            It confirms you transferred the value you actually rolled rather than one from an earlier
            session, which nothing downstream can tell on its own. It is the first four characters of
            the hash of the key, so a shell confirms it with nothing else installed:
        </p>
        <pre class="mono-block">printf '%s' '@backupKey.KeyHex' | sha256sum</pre>
        <p class="hint">
            The first four characters of that output are the check code.
        </p>

        @* The one thing this line must keep doing is separating "not a seed phrase" from "does
           nothing". It is a real key with a real job; it simply is not the thing you put in a
           wallet. Someone who reads it as decorative will not write it down. *@
        <p class="hint hint-loud">
            <strong>This is not a seed phrase.</strong> It does not go into a wallet and it will
            not restore anything on its own. It goes into <code>slip39-backup</code>, which
            encrypts your seed with it and splits it into shares.
        </p>
        <p class="hint">
            You do not need to keep this. Once <code>slip39-backup</code> has it, the shares are what
            reconstruct it. If you wrote it out to get it across, destroy that paper along with the
            roll log: both are the key in plain text, and a key that survives on paper defeats the
            shares entirely.
        </p>
    </div>
}

<h2>Checking this against another tool</h2>
<details class="runbook">
    <summary>The verification runbook</summary>

    @* EVERY COMMAND HERE CARRIES YOUR ACTUAL ROLLS, NOT A $ROLLS PLACEHOLDER.
       =====================================================================
       It used to use $ROLLS in five commands and define it in none. An unset variable expands to
       nothing, so anyone following it hashed the empty string and got e3b0c442...b855 from every
       command: a plausible hash matching nothing, whose obvious reading is that the app is wrong.
       That is the false alarm this page exists to prevent, produced by the page itself.

       Adding a "ROLLS=..." line first would have fixed it by instruction. Substituting the real
       log fixes it by construction: there is no variable to forget, and the line can go straight
       into a terminal. The log is on screen a few inches above anyway, so nothing is revealed
       here that the page was not already showing. *@
    <div class="step-label">Step 2 only: the hash, with two independent programs</div>
    <pre class="mono-block">printf '%s' '@RollsForCommands' | sha256sum
printf '%s' '@RollsForCommands' | openssl sha256</pre>
    <p class="hint">
        <strong>These print the hash and nothing else. They do not produce words</strong>, because
        neither program knows anything about BIP-39. What they check is step 2 above, which is the
        step where a wrong preimage shows up: a stray character, a separator, a trailing newline.
        Get the same hash and your rolls are being hashed identically; the words then follow from
        ordinary BIP-39, and the tools further down are what check that part.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
        Compare the hex and ignore the rest of the line. <code>sha256sum</code> appends
        <code>&nbsp;-</code> for standard input and <code>openssl</code> prints
        <code>SHA2-256(stdin)=</code> in front.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
        <code>printf '%s'</code>, never <code>echo</code>. A plain echo appends a newline,
        hashes a different string, and yields a different wallet.
    </p>

    @* ORDERED BY WHAT A NON-TECHNICAL READER CAN ACTUALLY DO.
       ======================================================
       It used to lead with the mnemonic package: pip3 download, unzip a wheel, set PYTHONPATH,
       paste a five-line Python program. That is the hardest option on the page and it was first,
       so anyone who is not comfortable in a terminal met a wall before reaching the option that
       needs no terminal at all. One check that gets done beats three that do not.

       Everything here has to survive one constraint: on Tails you cannot install anything, so
       "pip install" is not a step, it is a reason a tool is unusable. Every option is a file you
       carried on the stick. *@
    <div class="step-label">Pick one. Any of them is worth more than none</div>
    <p class="hint">
        Carry at least one of these on the stick before you boot, because Tails is amnesic and the
        network is off, so nothing can be downloaded or installed once you are there. They are
        listed easiest first.
    </p>

    <div class="step-label">1. No terminal: Ian Coleman's page</div>
    <p class="hint">
        One HTML file, opened by double-clicking it. Type your rolls into the Entropy box, set two
        dropdowns, and read the words. If you do only one cross-check, do this one. The settings
        are the next section, and they matter more than anything else on this page.
    </p>

    <div class="step-label">2. One command: Coldcard's rolls12.py</div>
    <pre class="mono-block">echo '@RollsForCommands' | python3 rolls12.py</pre>
    <p class="hint">
        One file, from Coldcard's verification docs. It imports nothing but <code>hashlib</code>
        and carries the wordlist inside it, so it needs no setup at all: copy it to the stick, open
        a terminal in the folder you copied it to, and run that line. It prints the truncated hash
        and the words.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
        Coldcard is the vendor whose entropy defect this app exists in response to. That defect was
        in firmware, not in this script, which is short enough to read end to end before you trust
        it. Read it, then use it.
    </p>

    <div class="step-label">3. If you are comfortable in a terminal: Trezor's reference library</div>
    <p class="hint">
        This is the implementation the official BIP-39 vectors are published alongside, so it is the
        strongest independent check available offline. It is also the most work. Four steps
        beforehand, on your ordinary networked computer:
    </p>
    <ol class="hint">
        <li>Open a terminal and run <code>pip3 download mnemonic --no-deps -d lib</code>. That
            downloads one file into a new folder called <code>lib</code> and installs nothing.</li>
        <li>Run <code>cd lib</code> then <code>unzip mnemonic-*.whl</code>. That unpacks it, and you
            now have a <code>mnemonic</code> folder inside <code>lib</code>.</li>
        <li>Copy the whole <code>lib</code> folder onto the USB stick, next to the AppImage.</li>
        <li>On Tails, copy <code>lib</code> from the stick into your Home folder, the same way you
            copied the AppImage.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="hint">
        Then open a terminal in your Home folder and paste this in one go, including the closing
        quote on the last line:
    </p>
    <pre class="mono-block">PYTHONPATH=lib python3 -c "
import hashlib
from mnemonic import Mnemonic
e = hashlib.sha256('@RollsForCommands'.encode()).digest()[:@EntropyByteCount]
print(e.hex())
print(Mnemonic('english').to_mnemonic(e))"</pre>
    <p class="hint">
        It prints the entropy and then the words. Nothing is installed and nothing touches the
        system: pointing <code>PYTHONPATH</code> at the folder is enough, because the package is
        pure Python. The two lines that matter are in plain sight, SHA-256 of the rolls truncated to
        @EntropyByteCount bytes, handed to a library that knows nothing about dice. If this and the
        app disagree, the app is wrong.
    </p>

    <div class="step-label">Not usable on Tails: SeedSigner's own tool</div>
    <p class="hint">
        SeedSigner ships <code>tools/mnemonic.py</code> specifically so its dice conversion can
        be checked, and it is built on <code>embit</code>, a third independent implementation.
        It needs <code>pip install</code> of both, so it is a check to run on a networked
        machine before the ceremony, not on Tails. Worth doing once; not something to plan the
        ceremony around.
    </p>

    <div class="step-label">Ian Coleman's page: the two settings that decide everything</div>
    <p class="hint">
        The most portable check of all: one file, opens from <code>file://</code>, so it needs
        no server, no proxy exception and no Python. It also has the two worst settings traps,
        both verified against v0.5.6 rather than taken on trust.
    </p>
    @* No hyperlink: this page makes no outbound request and an anchor invites a click on a machine
       that is supposed to be offline. The address is written out so it can be typed on the
       networked machine where the download belongs. *@
    <p class="hint">
        Download <code>bip39-standalone.html</code> from the releases page,
        <code>github.com/iancoleman/bip39/releases</code>, before you boot Tails. The current
        release is 0.5.6, which is the version the settings notes below were checked against.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
        That release also carries <code>signature.txt.asc</code>. You are about to check a seed
        against this file, so check the file first: verify the signature on the networked machine
        while you still can. A cross-check you have not verified is a second thing to trust, not a
        second opinion.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
        Set <strong>Mnemonic Length to 12 or 24, never "raw"</strong>. Only the numeric lengths
        take the SHA-256 path. With "raw" the same fifty 1s produced
        <em>empty cause myself empty cause myself empty cause myself empty cause negative</em>,
        which at least announces itself by repeating.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
        Set entropy type to <strong>Base 10, not "Dice"</strong>. The Dice type rewrites every
        6 to a 0 before hashing. With a real log this is silent and total: fifty rolls
        beginning 655152... gave <em>hole luggage safe present...</em> under Base 10 and
        <em>tip toward alcohol rule pave...</em> under Dice. A different wallet, no warning.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
        <strong>Test your settings with a log that contains at least one 6.</strong> Dice and
        Base 10 differ only where a 6 appears, so a log of all 1s produces the same words under
        both and proves nothing about the setting that matters. The easiest log to type is the
        one that cannot catch this.
    </p>
    @* The setting can be checked on his own page instead of taken on trust from ours, and that is
       worth more than another paragraph of assertion. His Filtered Entropy field is the string the
       tool actually consumes, so the rewrite is visible there before any hashing happens. *@
    <p class="hint">
        <strong>You can watch this happen, rather than take our word for it.</strong> Tick
        <em>Show entropy details</em> and look at the <strong>Filtered Entropy</strong> line: that
        is the string his tool actually uses. Type a log with a 6 in it and switch between the two
        settings. Under Base 10 the line matches what you typed. Under Dice, every 6 becomes a
        <code>0</code>, and the words change underneath it.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
        Why his Dice setting does that, since it is not a defect: treating rolls as base-6 digits
        needs the values 0 to 5, and 6 is congruent to 0. It is the correct conversion for that
        interpretation. It is the wrong one here, because Coldcard, SeedSigner and Krux do not treat
        d6 rolls as base-6 digits at all. They hash the literal characters, which is what Base 10
        leaves alone and what the preimage above shows.
    </p>

    <div class="step-label">On a SeedSigner</div>
    <p class="hint">
        Tools, New Seed, dice. 50 rolls for 12 words and 99 for 24, the same minimums this app
        uses. SeedSigner hashes the bare digit string exactly as shown above, so the preimage
        on this page is the string it hashes.
    </p>

    <div class="step-label">On a Coldcard</div>
    <p class="hint">
        Firmware 5.6.0 or later, and read that requirement as more than housekeeping: firmware
        before it did not call the hardware random number generator during seed generation for
        five years, which is the defect this app exists in response to. Dice Only mode never
        used the RNG, so a dice-derived seed is not affected, but there is no reason to run
        known-bad firmware.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
        Choose <strong>12 Word Dice Roll</strong> and <strong>Dice Only</strong>, not TRNG plus
        Dice: the mixed mode blends device entropy that cannot be reproduced, so this app will
        legitimately disagree and it will look like a bug. Enter exactly the minimum number of
        rolls; below it the device tops up from its own generator without making that obvious.
        The zero-roll screen shows <code>e3b0c442...b855</code>, the SHA-256 of the empty
        string, which confirms the hash display works before any real digit is entered.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
        Coldcard's <code>rolls12.py</code>, from its verification docs, is still a useful
        second opinion despite the above: it is a short script you can read in full, and the
        defect was in firmware, not in it. The command is in the runbook above, option 2.
    </p>

    <div class="step-label">Do not do this on a networked machine</div>
    <p class="hint">
        Coldcard's own documentation states that entering a real roll log on a networked
        general-purpose computer completely compromises the device's security.
    </p>
</details>

<footer>
    @* The version is on the page because there is otherwise no way to tell which build you are
       looking at, and that matters most where it is least visible: the demo redeploys on every
       push to main, so it runs ahead of the newest release as soon as anything merges. A bare
       "1.1.0" would then be a claim the build does not support.

       So the commit is shown next to it. The .NET SDK puts it in the informational version as
       "1.1.0+<sha>" on its own, from the git checkout, with no CI flag needed. Twelve characters
       is git's own long-form abbreviation and enough to find the commit; the full hash is in the
       title attribute for anyone comparing exactly.

       A release is the version with nothing after it to worry about: check the commit against the
       tag. If they differ, this build is not that release. *@
    <p>
        Version <code>@releaseVersion</code>
        @* A span, not a <text> block: Razor strips leading whitespace from <text>, which rendered
           "1.1.0at commit". Element content keeps it. *@
        @if (buildCommit.Length > 0)
        {
            <span> at commit <code title="@buildCommit">@buildCommit[..Math.Min(12, buildCommit.Length)]</code></span>
        }
    </p>
    <p>
        BIP-39 English wordlist, 2048 words, SHA-256
        <code>@wordListHash</code>@(wordListOk ? " (verified at startup)" : " (CHECK FAILED)")
    </p>
    <p>
        @* "No clipboard writes" used to sit here as though it were a safeguard. It is not: you can
           select any of this and copy it yourself. What is true, and worth saying, is that the app
           never does it for you, so nothing lands on the clipboard unless you put it there. *@
        No storage, no network, no telemetry. Nothing is copied for you: whatever reaches your
        clipboard, you put there. No random number generator:
        every bit of entropy here came off your dice.
    </p>
</footer>

</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// What the rolls currently on screen are for. Not a preference and not stored: it decides
    /// which derivation runs and what the result is, and it is rendered on the page throughout
    /// rather than being something the user has to remember choosing.
    /// </summary>
    @* DerivationMode and the switching rule live in DiceToSeed.Core/ModeSwitch.cs, not here.
       They were here, and the rule that one roll log must never produce both a seed and a key was
       four lines of view code that no test would have noticed the removal of. See ModeSwitchTests. *@

    // The whole roll log: one character per roll, appended only by RecordRoll. It lives here
    // and dies with the tab; nothing is stored anywhere.
    string rolls = string.Empty;
    WordCount wordCount = WordCount.Twelve;
    DerivationMode mode = DerivationMode.SeedPhrase;

    // Only one of these is ever set, because only one mode can have been run. RunDerivation
    // clears both before deciding, so a stale result from the other mode cannot survive on
    // screen next to a log that no longer produced it.
    SeedDerivation? derivation;
    BackupKeyDerivation? backupKey;

    // Set only while a mode switch is waiting on the user, because switching discards rolls.
    DerivationMode? pendingMode;

    string? error;


    string host = string.Empty;
    bool servedFromElsewhere;
    string wordListHash = string.Empty;
    bool wordListOk;

    // The two halves of the informational version, "1.1.0" and the commit after the '+'. The
    // commit is empty when the build had no git checkout to read, which is what a source download
    // gives you, and the line then simply shows the version.
    string releaseVersion = string.Empty;
    string buildCommit = string.Empty;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        // Read the origin from the navigation URI rather than through JS interop: one less moving
        // part, and no JavaScript file to review.
        //
        // The decision and its wording both come from Core, where they are tested. This used to be
        // an inline expression comparing the host against three strings, and the Pages workflow
        // claimed the behaviour was tested when nothing tested it. See ServingOrigin for the cases
        // that matter, including 127.0.0.1.example.com, which a substring check would have passed.
        var uri = new Uri(Navigation.Uri);
        host = ServingOrigin.Describe(uri);
        servedFromElsewhere = !ServingOrigin.IsLocal(uri);

        // Read from the assembly rather than from a constant, so the number on screen is the one
        // the build actually carries. Directory.Build.props reads it from the VERSION file, and
        // VersionTests asserts the two agree and that the shape is "version" or "version+commit".
        var informational = Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly()
            .GetCustomAttribute<AssemblyInformationalVersionAttribute>()?.InformationalVersion
            ?? "unknown";

        var plus = informational.IndexOf('+');
        releaseVersion = plus < 0 ? informational : informational[..plus];
        buildCommit = plus < 0 ? string.Empty : informational[(plus + 1)..];

        // The wordlist check runs once, at startup, and its outcome is on screen either way.
        // A failure here disables derivation entirely: see CanDerive.
        var wordList = Bip39WordList.Load();
        wordListOk = wordList.IsSuccess;
        wordListHash = wordListOk ? wordList.Value.Sha256Hex : "unavailable";

        if (wordList.IsFailure)
            error = wordList.Error;
    }

    static readonly IReadOnlyList<int> Faces = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];

    // The rows of the entropy table: the two vendor minimums and the two recommendations, each
    // against the target it is meant to clear. Built from RollEntropy rather than typed out, so
    // the table and the counter can never quote different numbers.
    static readonly IReadOnlyList<(int Count, int Target, string Label)> EntropyRows =
    [
        (DiceRolls.MinimumRollsFor(WordCount.Twelve), RollEntropy.TargetBitsFor(WordCount.Twelve), "(12-word minimum)"),
        (RollEntropy.RecommendedRollsFor(WordCount.Twelve), RollEntropy.TargetBitsFor(WordCount.Twelve), "(12-word recommended)"),
        (DiceRolls.MinimumRollsFor(WordCount.TwentyFour), RollEntropy.TargetBitsFor(WordCount.TwentyFour), "(24-word minimum)"),
        (RollEntropy.RecommendedRollsFor(WordCount.TwentyFour), RollEntropy.TargetBitsFor(WordCount.TwentyFour), "(24-word recommended)"),
    ];

    /// <summary>
    /// Appends one roll. The value comes from the button the user pressed after reading a
    /// physical die; nothing here chooses it.
    ///
    /// Every mutation of the log also drops any result on screen. Leaving previous words
    /// visible above a log that has since changed is the one way this page could actively
    /// mislead: a glance down after a correction would read words belonging to a log that no
    /// longer exists.
    /// </summary>
    void RecordRoll(int face)
    {
        rolls += face.ToString();
        ClearResults();
    }

    void UndoLastRoll()
    {
        rolls = rolls.Length == 0 ? rolls : rolls[..^1];
        ClearResults();
    }

    void ClearRolls()
    {
        rolls = string.Empty;
        ClearResults();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Both results and the error, dropped together. Which one was on screen depends on the mode
    /// that produced it, and neither may outlive the log it came from.
    /// </summary>
    void ClearResults()
    {
        derivation = null;
        backupKey = null;
        error = null;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// 1 to 6 record a roll, Backspace undoes the last. Nothing else does anything: there is
    /// no text field on this page, so there is no keystroke to interpret as text.
    /// </summary>
    void HandleKey(KeyboardEventArgs args)
    {
        if (args.Key.Length == 1 && args.Key[0] is >= '1' and <= '6')
            RecordRoll(args.Key[0] - '0');
        else if (args.Key == "Backspace")
            UndoLastRoll();
    }

    // The log can only ever hold digits 1 to 6, because those are the only values the buttons
    // append. Core is still asked to validate it: this page is one caller of a library that
    // must refuse bad input on its own account, and the check costs nothing.
    Result<DiceRollLog> ParsedLog => DiceRolls.Read(rolls);

    int RollCount => rolls.Length;

    // The runbook's commands carry the log actually on screen, so a line can go straight into a
    // terminal with nothing to substitute. Before any rolls exist they name what belongs there
    // rather than showing a made-up log, which someone could run and then wonder why the words
    // disagree with the app.
    /// <summary>
    /// The printed sheet for the word count in effect. There are two, sixty boxes and a hundred and
    /// eleven, because one sheet covering both had to say which of two heavier lines applied to whom,
    /// and that sentence was confusing. The page already knows which count you are rolling, so it
    /// links the right sheet rather than handing over a choice it has already made.
    /// </summary>
    string SheetBaseName =>
        wordCount == WordCount.Twelve ? "roll-sheet-12-words" : "roll-sheet-24-words";

    string RollsForCommands => LivePreimage.Length > 0 ? LivePreimage : "YOUR-ROLLS-GO-HERE";

    // 16 for twelve words, 32 for twenty-four. The runbook's Python slices to this, and stating it
    // rather than hardcoding 16 with a comment about 24 removes the one edit a reader could get
    // wrong while copying.
    int EntropyByteCount => (int)DiceRolls.StrengthFor(wordCount) / 8;

    string LivePreimage => ParsedLog.IsSuccess ? ParsedLog.Value.Preimage : string.Empty;

    // The backup key never touches the wordlist, and is still blocked when the wordlist check
    // fails. CLAUDE.md rule 7 says the app refuses to derive anything, and that reading is the
    // right one: a wordlist that does not match its published hash means the files being served
    // are not the files that were published, which is a reason to distrust the whole build
    // rather than only the part that would have used them.
    //
    // The minimum is asked in word-count terms in both modes because it is provably the same
    // number; BackupKeyTests pins that so the two cannot drift apart.
    bool CanDerive => wordListOk && ParsedLog.IsSuccess && ParsedLog.Value.MeetsMinimumFor(wordCount).IsSuccess;

    // An empty log is where everyone starts, not a mistake, so it reads 0 with an instruction
    // rather than an error. Core still returns a failure for empty input, which is right for a
    // caller asking to derive; the difference is only in how a page that has just loaded
    // presents it.
    bool NothingRecordedYet => rolls.Length == 0;

    // THE COUNTER TARGETS THE RECOMMENDED COUNT, NOT THE VENDOR MINIMUM.
    // ==================================================================
    // The common use of this app is making a new seed and then checking it against a second
    // implementation, so the number the counter drives you toward is the one a new seed should
    // have: 60 rolls for 12 words, 111 for 24. See RollEntropy for where those come from.
    //
    // Derive still unlocks at the vendor minimum, 50 and 99, because reproducing a device's
    // 50-roll seed is the other thing this app is for and gating it would turn away exactly the
    // people the 2026 Coldcard advisory sent here. So the minimum is a floor on what is possible
    // and the recommendation is a target on screen, and the note says which is which.
    int RecommendedRolls => RollEntropy.RecommendedRollsFor(wordCount);

    string CounterClass =>
        NothingRecordedYet ? "counter-under"
        : RollCount == RecommendedRolls ? "counter-exact"
        : RollCount > RecommendedRolls ? "counter-over"
        // Past the vendor floor but short of the recommendation: derivable, not yet what a new
        // seed wants. Amber rather than green says that without blocking anything.
        : RollCount >= DiceRolls.MinimumRollsFor(wordCount) ? "counter-over"
        : "counter-under";

    /// <summary>
    /// A result derived from fewer rolls than a new seed should have. Not an error: the count is
    /// above the vendor minimum or nothing would have derived at all. It is shown with the result
    /// rather than as a confirmation before it, because a dialog you click through to reach what
    /// you came for is a dialog nobody reads, while a line sitting next to the words stays there
    /// while they are written down.
    /// </summary>
    bool ShortOfRecommended =>
        (derivation is not null || backupKey is not null) && RollCount < RecommendedRolls;

    string RecommendationWarning
    {
        get
        {
            var minimum = DiceRolls.MinimumRollsFor(wordCount);
            var target = RollEntropy.TargetBitsFor(wordCount);
            var thing = mode == DerivationMode.BackupKey ? "key" : "seed";

            var reproducing = $"Derived from {RollCount} rolls. If you are reproducing a {minimum}-roll {thing} that already exists, this is right and you should not add rolls.";

            var shortfall = RollEntropy.MinimumFallsShortFor(wordCount)
                ? $"{minimum} fair rolls carry {RollEntropy.FairBits(minimum):0.0} bits and the target is {target}, so the vendor minimum does not reach it even with perfect dice."
                : $"{RollCount} fair rolls carry {RollEntropy.FairBits(RollCount):0.0} bits against a target of {target}, and on the conservative measure a die with one face at a fifth carries only {RollEntropy.PessimisticBits(RollCount):0.0}.";

            return $"{reproducing} If this is a new {thing}, roll {RecommendedRolls} instead: {shortfall}";
        }
    }

    string CounterNote
    {
        get
        {
            var minimum = DiceRolls.MinimumRollsFor(wordCount);
            var target = RollEntropy.TargetBitsFor(wordCount);
            var recommended = RecommendedRolls;

            var thing = mode == DerivationMode.BackupKey ? $"{target}-bit key" : $"{(int)wordCount}-word seed";

            if (NothingRecordedYet)
                return $"Roll a real die and press the face you rolled: {recommended} rolls for a new {thing}.";

            if (RollCount < minimum)
                return $"{minimum - RollCount} more before this can derive at all, {recommended - RollCount} more for the {recommended} a new {thing} should have.";

            if (RollCount < recommended)
                // The honest statement of what the vendor minimum buys. At 24 words it does not
                // even reach the target on perfect dice, which is the fact this copy exists for.
                return RollEntropy.MinimumFallsShortFor(wordCount)
                    ? $"Enough to reproduce a device's {minimum}-roll seed. Not enough for a new one: {minimum} fair rolls carry {RollEntropy.FairBits(minimum):0.0} bits and the target is {target}, so the vendor minimum does not reach it even with perfect dice. {recommended - RollCount} more for {recommended}."
                    : $"Enough to reproduce a device's {minimum}-roll seed, and thin for a new one: {minimum} fair rolls carry {RollEntropy.FairBits(minimum):0.0} bits against a target of {target}, and a real die carries {RollEntropy.MeasuredBits(minimum):0.0}. {recommended - RollCount} more for {recommended}.";

            if (RollCount == recommended)
                // The margin, said with the size of it rather than an assurance. "Worse than real
                // dice are" stood here on its own for two releases, which is the kind of sentence
                // this app is supposed to be checkable instead of.
                return $"At {recommended}. That clears {target} bits even if one face of your die comes up a fifth of the time, which is {RollEntropy.PessimismFactor:0} times more lopsided than the largest bias ever measured on ordinary dice.";

            return mode == DerivationMode.BackupKey
                ? $"{RollCount - recommended} above {recommended}. All {RollCount} rolls are hashed, and the key is 32 bytes at any length."
                // Nothing is discarded above the recommendation, and it does not raise the seed's
                // strength either: the entropy is a truncation of SHA-256, fixed at the target
                // however long the log is. The device warning is the part that bites, because a
                // log differing from the one entered on the hardware wallet gives a completely
                // different seed and looks exactly like the device ignoring the dice.
                : $"{RollCount - recommended} above {recommended}. All {RollCount} rolls are hashed. The seed stays {target} bits; the extra rolls are margin against unfair dice. If you are also entering these on a device, enter all {RollCount} or the seeds will not match.";
        }
    }

    void SelectWordCount(WordCount selected)
    {
        wordCount = selected;
        ClearResults();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Asks for a mode. With rolls already recorded this only raises the confirmation, because
    /// switching clears them: one roll log must never yield both a seed phrase and a backup key.
    /// On a 24-word seed the entropy is byte for byte the key, so a log used twice makes k
    /// identical to the wallet it protects. Clearing on switch is what makes that unreachable
    /// from this page rather than merely discouraged in the documentation.
    /// </summary>
    void RequestMode(DerivationMode requested)
    {
        if (ModeSwitch.RequiresConfirmation(mode, requested, rolls.Length))
        {
            pendingMode = requested;
            return;
        }

        EnterMode(requested);
    }

    void ConfirmModeSwitch()
    {
        if (pendingMode is { } requested)
            EnterMode(requested);
    }

    void CancelModeSwitch() => pendingMode = null;

    /// <summary>
    /// Applies a mode, and the clearing decision is not made here: <see cref="ModeSwitch.Apply"/>
    /// returns the log that survives the transition, which is nothing whenever the mode changed.
    /// Assigning both from one call is deliberate, so that the mode cannot end up changed while a
    /// log from the other purpose is still recorded.
    /// </summary>
    void EnterMode(DerivationMode requested)
    {
        (mode, rolls) = ModeSwitch.Apply(mode, requested, rolls);
        pendingMode = null;
        ClearResults();
    }

    static string ModeName(DerivationMode value) =>
        value == DerivationMode.SeedPhrase ? "seed phrase" : "backup key";

    // The whole page draws its accent from one custom property, so the mode is applied by
    // redefining that property on the wrapper rather than by colouring each control.
    string ModeClass => mode == DerivationMode.SeedPhrase ? "for-seed" : "for-key";

    /// <summary>
    /// One button, two derivations, and the results cleared first so a failure cannot leave the
    /// previous mode's answer sitting under a log that no longer produced it.
    /// </summary>
    void RunDerivation()
    {
        ClearResults();

        if (mode == DerivationMode.SeedPhrase)
        {
            var result = DiceSeed.Derive(rolls, wordCount);

            derivation = result.IsSuccess ? result.Value : null;
            error = result.IsFailure ? result.Error : null;
        }
        else
        {
            var result = BackupKey.Derive(rolls, DiceRolls.StrengthFor(wordCount));

            backupKey = result.IsSuccess ? result.Value : null;
            error = result.IsFailure ? result.Error : null;
        }
    }
}
